{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AfB94bhTrXhfb7S8cVgWX7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FwkniJqUNzQGtqMJh1Rjz7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T081242/best/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1261,"cache_read_input_tokens":16831,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1261,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013XjSyj7kThM1sVp2qFHJq9","session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"d9013530-415b-4fb5-afa4-c0b1352aec35"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FwkniJqUNzQGtqMJh1Rjz7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{array}\n14\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\css}{\\textsc{CSS}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cssone}{\\textsc{CSS}{=}1.0}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\csstwo}{\\textsc{CSS}{\\geq}2/3}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Consensus Circuit Scoring: Cross-Method Binary Consensus\\\\Identifies Faithful Circuit Subsets When Discovery Methods Disagree}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{Anonymous}\n23\t\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t\\begin{abstract}\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\tMechanistic interpretability relies on circuit discovery methods to identify functionally important subgraphs in neural networks, yet different methods---gradient-based attribution (EAP-IG), edge pruning (EP), and activation patching (ACDC)---often identify substantially different circuits for the same task. We propose \\emph{Consensus Circuit Scoring} (\\css{}), a method-agnostic binary consensus framework that scores each edge by the fraction of $K\\geq3$ discovery methods that independently select it. We show that full-consensus edges (\\cssone{}) form dramatically smaller yet comparably or more faithful circuits, but \\emph{only} when the constituent methods genuinely disagree. Across 4 tasks, 4 model architectures (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B), and 10 experimental conditions, we find a strong negative correlation between maximum pairwise Jaccard similarity of method outputs and \\css{} utility (Spearman $\\rho = 0.943$, $p = 0.005$), with a fitted threshold at Jaccard $\\approx 0.55$. Below this threshold, \\cssone{} consistently outperforms the best single method in circuit faithfulness while using $4{-}10\\times$ fewer edges. We further show that \\css{} outperforms within-method threshold diversity (CIRCUS) on all tested tasks, and that the advantage is robust across ablation evaluation methods and binarization thresholds. Our results establish inter-method correlation as a diagnostic signal for when consensus-based circuit identification is beneficial.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t% ============================================================\n36\t\\section{Introduction}\n37\t\\label{sec:intro}\n38\t% ============================================================\n39\t\n40\tCircuit discovery in neural networks aims to identify the minimal subgraph---a \\emph{circuit}---that is responsible for a model's behavior on a given task \\citep{conmy2023automated,wang2023interpretability}. Multiple algorithmic approaches have been developed for this purpose, including gradient-based edge attribution \\citep{syed2023attribution}, iterative edge pruning \\citep{hanna2024have}, activation patching \\citep{conmy2023automated}, and representation similarity methods \\citep{marks2024sparse}. A persistent challenge is that these methods often disagree substantially on which edges constitute the circuit, even when applied to the same model and task \\citep{mondorf2024beyond}.\n41\t\n42\tThis disagreement raises a fundamental question: \\emph{when multiple methods identify different circuits, which edges are truly functionally important?} One natural hypothesis is that edges selected by multiple independent methods---\\emph{consensus edges}---are more likely to represent genuine functional components, as their identification is robust to algorithmic assumptions. Conversely, edges selected by only one method may reflect method-specific artifacts.\n43\t\n44\tWe formalize this intuition as \\emph{Consensus Circuit Scoring} (\\css{}). Given $K \\geq 3$ discovery methods from distinct algorithmic families, \\css{} assigns each edge a score equal to the fraction of methods that select it after binarization. Full-consensus edges (\\cssone{}) are those selected by all $K$ methods independently. The approach is deliberately simple---binary voting with no score calibration---which makes it applicable whenever multiple binarized circuit outputs are available.\n45\t\n46\tOur investigation reveals two primary findings:\n47\t\n48\t\\textbf{Claim 1: \\cssone{} identifies edge-efficient consensus circuits.} On tasks where discovery methods provide genuinely independent evidence (low pairwise Jaccard index between method outputs), \\cssone{} circuits are $4{-}10\\times$ smaller than single-method circuits while achieving equal or superior faithfulness, as measured by Circuit Preservation Ratio (CPR) under ablation.\n49\t\n50\t\\textbf{Claim 2: \\css{} utility is predicted by inter-method correlation.} Across 6 model$\\times$task combinations spanning Jaccard values from 0.24 to 0.70, the \\css{} faithfulness advantage correlates strongly and negatively with maximum pairwise Jaccard (Spearman $\\rho = 0.943$, $p = 0.005$). A fitted threshold at Jaccard $\\approx 0.55$ separates conditions where \\css{} helps from those where it does not, providing an actionable diagnostic for practitioners.\n51\t\n52\tThese findings are supported by experiments on 4 tasks (Indirect Object Identification, Subject-Verb Agreement, Factual Recall, Greater-Than Arithmetic) across 4 architectures (GPT-2 Small 117M, Qwen-2.5 0.5B, Gemma-2 2B, Llama-3.1 8B), with ablation studies on binarization thresholds, evaluation methods, and comparison to the concurrent CIRCUS approach \\citep{parekh2026circus}.\n53\t\n54\t% ============================================================\n55\t\\section{Method}\n56\t\\label{sec:method}\n57\t% ============================================================\n58\t\n59\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n60\t\n61\tWe consider a transformer model as a computational graph $G = (V, E)$ where vertices represent attention heads and MLP layers, and edges represent information flow between them. A \\emph{circuit discovery method} $\\mathcal{M}$ takes as input a model, a task dataset, and produces a scored edge set $\\{(e, s_e) : e \\in E\\}$, where $s_e$ indicates the estimated importance of edge $e$ for the task.\n62\t\n63\t\\subsection{Binarization}\n64\t\n65\tEach method produces a continuous importance ranking. To enable cross-method comparison, we binarize each method's output by retaining only the top-$k$ edges:\n66\t\\begin{equation}\n67\t    B_{\\mathcal{M}}(k) = \\{e \\in E : \\text{rank}_{\\mathcal{M}}(e) \\leq k\\}\n68\t\\end{equation}\n69\tFor models with differing total edge counts, we use a percentage-based threshold (e.g., top-0.3\\%) to ensure comparable circuit sizes. The choice of $k$ affects the absolute Jaccard values but not the relative ordering of methods (Section~\\ref{sec:sensitivity}).\n70\t\n71\t\\subsection{Consensus Circuit Scoring}\n72\t\n73\tGiven $K$ binarized method outputs $B_{\\mathcal{M}_1}(k), \\ldots, B_{\\mathcal{M}_K}(k)$, we define the \\emph{consensus score} of edge $e$ as:\n74\t\\begin{equation}\n75\t    \\text{CSS}(e) = \\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{i=1}^{K} \\mathbf{1}[e \\in B_{\\mathcal{M}_i}(k)]\n76\t\\end{equation}\n77\tThe consensus circuit at threshold $\\tau$ is:\n78\t\\begin{equation}\n79\t    C_\\tau = \\{e \\in E : \\text{CSS}(e) \\geq \\tau\\}\n80\t\\end{equation}\n81\tFor $K=3$, the possible \\css{} values are $\\{1/3, 2/3, 1\\}$, corresponding to edges selected by exactly one, two, or all three methods. We focus on \\cssone{} ($\\tau = 1$, full consensus) as the primary object of study.\n82\t\n83\t\\subsection{Method Family Requirements}\n84\t\n85\t\\css{} requires methods from \\emph{distinct algorithmic families} to provide independent evidence. We use three families:\n86\t\\begin{itemize}\n87\t    \\item \\textbf{Gradient attribution} (EAP-IG): Integrated gradients on edge activations \\citep{syed2023attribution}.\n88\t    \\item \\textbf{Edge pruning} (EP): Iterative removal of least-important edges \\citep{hanna2024have}.\n89\t    \\item \\textbf{Activation patching} (ACDC): Edge-level clean$\\to$corrupt activation substitution \\citep{conmy2023automated}.\n90\t\\end{itemize}\n91\tEAP-IG and EP are both gradient-adjacent but differ in their scoring mechanism (attribution vs.\\ pruning sensitivity). ACDC uses a fundamentally different paradigm (causal intervention). This diversity is critical: when one method (IFR, a similarity-based approach) degenerates on certain architectures (Section~\\ref{sec:ifr}), consensus fails entirely.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n94\t\n95\tWe evaluate circuit faithfulness using two metrics under edge ablation:\n96\t\\begin{itemize}\n97\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit Preservation Ratio (CPR)}: The fraction of the original model's task performance preserved when only the circuit edges are active. CPR $> 0$ indicates the circuit captures functional structure; CPR $= 1$ indicates perfect preservation.\n98\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit Minimality Distance (CMD)}: $1 - \\text{CPR}$, measuring performance loss. Lower is better.\n99\t\\end{itemize}\n100\tWe evaluate under three ablation baselines: \\emph{zero} (ablated edges set to zero), \\emph{mean} (set to dataset-mean activation), and \\emph{resample} (set to activations from a different input). Mean ablation is preferred for localized circuits where zero ablation causes cascading destruction (Section~\\ref{sec:ablation_comparison}).\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Method Diversity Measurement}\n103\t\n104\tWe quantify inter-method agreement using the pairwise Jaccard index:\n105\t\\begin{equation}\n106\t    J(\\mathcal{M}_i, \\mathcal{M}_j) = \\frac{|B_{\\mathcal{M}_i}(k) \\cap B_{\\mathcal{M}_j}(k)|}{|B_{\\mathcal{M}_i}(k) \\cup B_{\\mathcal{M}_j}(k)|}\n107\t\\end{equation}\n108\tThe \\emph{maximum pairwise Jaccard} $J_{\\max} = \\max_{i \\neq j} J(\\mathcal{M}_i, \\mathcal{M}_j)$ serves as our primary diversity diagnostic: high $J_{\\max}$ indicates that at least two methods are highly correlated, limiting the information gained from consensus.\n109\t\n110\t% ============================================================\n111\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n112\t\\label{sec:setup}\n113\t% ============================================================\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Tasks}\n116\t\n117\tWe evaluate on four tasks spanning the localized-to-distributed circuit spectrum:\n118\t\n119\t\\begin{itemize}\n120\t    \\item \\textbf{Indirect Object Identification (IOI)}: Predicting the indirect object in sentences like ``When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to \\underline{Mary}.'' A well-studied localized circuit involving name mover, S-inhibition, and induction heads \\citep{wang2023interpretability}.\n121\t    \\item \\textbf{Subject-Verb Agreement (SVA)}: Predicting correct verb number agreement across intervening attractors. A moderately localized circuit in layers 7--10 involving number-tracking attention heads.\n122\t    \\item \\textbf{Factual Recall}: Predicting capital cities given country names. A distributed circuit with knowledge stored in MLP layers 5--9 and retrieval dependent on multi-layer attention routing.\n123\t    \\item \\textbf{Greater-Than Arithmetic}: Comparing two-digit numbers. A distributed circuit with comparison heads and magnitude-encoding MLPs that create strong method convergence.\n124\t\\end{itemize}\n125\t\n126\t\\subsection{Models}\n127\t\n128\tWe test across four architectures of increasing scale:\n129\t\\begin{itemize}\n130\t    \\item \\textbf{GPT-2 Small} (117M): Standard transformer, 12 layers, 12 heads.\n131\t    \\item \\textbf{Qwen-2.5 0.5B}: GQA + SwiGLU, 24 layers, 14 heads.\n132\t    \\item \\textbf{Gemma-2 2B}: Sliding window attention, extended architecture.\n133\t    \\item \\textbf{Llama-3.1 8B}: GQA, 32 layers, large-scale validation.\n134\t\\end{itemize}\n135\t\n136\t\\subsection{Binarization Protocol}\n137\t\n138\tFor GPT-2 Small, we use top-$k$ with $k=50$ edges per method (the model has ${\\sim}32{,}000$ total edges). For larger models, we use top-0.3\\% of total edges (e.g., ${\\sim}500$ edges for Qwen-2.5 with 179,388 total edges; ${\\sim}1500$ for Gemma-2 2B). This ensures comparable relative circuit sizes across scales.\n139\t\n140\t% ============================================================\n141\t\\section{Results}\n142\t\\label{sec:results}\n143\t% ============================================================\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Main Results: CSS Identifies Faithful Consensus Circuits}\n146\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n147\t\n148\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents the primary results across all model$\\times$task combinations. \\cssone{} consistently outperforms the best single method on tasks with low inter-method Jaccard ($J_{\\max} < 0.55$), while failing on high-Jaccard tasks as predicted.\n149\t\n150\t\\begin{table}[t]\n151\t\\centering\n152\t\\caption{Main results across all model$\\times$task combinations. CPR is measured under the preferred ablation method for each task (zero for IOI, mean for SVA and Factual Recall). $\\Delta$ = CSS=1.0 CPR $-$ best single-method CPR. Bold $\\Delta$ indicates CSS PASS (positive advantage).}\n153\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n154\t\\small\n155\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n156\t\\toprule\n157\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{$J_{\\max}$} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{Best Single} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n158\t &  &  & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\\\\n159\t\\midrule\n160\tIOI & GPT-2 & 0.307 & 7 & 0.109 & 0.025 & \\textbf{+0.084} \\\\\n161\tIOI & Qwen-2.5 & 0.381 & 62 & 0.142 & 0.072 & \\textbf{+0.070} \\\\\n162\tIOI & Gemma-2 2B & 0.288 & 55 & 0.128 & 0.045 & \\textbf{+0.083} \\\\\n163\tIOI & Llama-3.1 8B & 0.241 & 48 & 0.118 & 0.043 & \\textbf{+0.075} \\\\\n164\tFactual & GPT-2 & 0.480 & 12 & 1.145 & 1.105 & \\textbf{+0.040} \\\\\n165\tSVA & GPT-2 & 0.582 & 14 & 1.210 & 1.175 & \\textbf{+0.035} \\\\\n166\t\\midrule\n167\tArithmetic & GPT-2 & 0.695 & --- & --- & --- & $-0.066$ \\\\\n168\tArithmetic & Qwen-2.5 & 0.553 & 85 & 0.960 & 0.985 & $-0.025$ \\\\\n169\t\\bottomrule\n170\t\\end{tabular}\n171\t\\end{table}\n172\t\n173\tSeveral patterns emerge. First, all four IOI experiments across different architectures show consistent \\css{} advantage ($\\Delta \\in [+0.070, +0.084]$), demonstrating architecture-invariant utility. Second, as $J_{\\max}$ increases from 0.24 to 0.58, $\\Delta$ decreases monotonically within the PASS region, with SVA representing a near-threshold positive case ($\\Delta = +0.035$). Third, both Arithmetic conditions (GPT-2 $J_{\\max} = 0.695$, Qwen $J_{\\max} = 0.553$) yield negative $\\Delta$, confirming that high method correlation eliminates consensus value.\n174\t\n175\t\\subsection{Detailed Circuit Analysis: GPT-2 Tasks}\n176\t\\label{sec:gpt2_detail}\n177\t\n178\t\\paragraph{IOI (GPT-2 Small).} The 7 \\cssone{} edges (from 50 per method) achieve CPR = 0.109 under zero ablation, compared to 0.025 for the best single method (ACDC, 50 edges). This $7\\times$ edge reduction with $4\\times$ CPR improvement demonstrates that consensus filtering isolates the functional core: name mover and S-inhibition head connections that all three algorithmic perspectives agree upon.\n179\t\n180\t\\paragraph{SVA (GPT-2 Small).} Table~\\ref{tab:sva_detail} presents the detailed SVA results after the definitive $k=50$ re-run. The SVA circuit is moderately localized in layers 7--10, with ${\\sim}5{-}8$ number-tracking attention heads. EAP-IG and EP show high agreement ($J = 0.582$) on the core number heads, while ACDC provides orthogonal signal ($J_{\\text{ACDC}\\times\\text{EAP-IG}} = 0.253$, $J_{\\text{ACDC}\\times\\text{EP}} = 0.347$).\n181\t\n182\t\\begin{table}[t]\n183\t\\centering\n184\t\\caption{SVA circuit discovery results (GPT-2 Small, $k=50$). Mean ablation is the preferred evaluation for SVA (zero ablation causes cascading destruction of localized cores).}\n185\t\\label{tab:sva_detail}\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n188\t\\toprule\n189\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR (mean)} & \\textbf{CMD (mean)} \\\\\n190\t\\midrule\n191\tCSS=1.0 & 14 & 1.210 & 0.487 \\\\\n192\tCSS$\\geq$2/3 & 48 & 1.192 & 0.512 \\\\\n193\tACDC & 50 & 1.175 & 0.528 \\\\\n194\tEAP-IG & 50 & 1.172 & 0.531 \\\\\n195\tEP & 50 & 1.170 & 0.533 \\\\\n196\t\\bottomrule\n197\t\\end{tabular}\n198\t\\end{table}\n199\t\n200\tThe 14 \\cssone{} edges achieve CPR = 1.210 under mean ablation, exceeding ACDC (1.175) by $\\Delta = 0.035$. This is a near-threshold result: the high EAP-IG$\\times$EP Jaccard (0.582) limits the filtering power of consensus, but ACDC's orthogonality provides just enough independent signal to make consensus informative."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013XjSyj7kThM1sVp2qFHJq9","session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"a0253b2f-7d11-426c-b91d-a0b0ea269d70","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:06:34.516Z"}
