{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013ZJuxc3zFDLjKhCteLCX2C","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tThe 14 \\cssone{} edges achieve CPR = 1.210 under mean ablation, exceeding ACDC (1.175) by $\\Delta = 0.035$. This is a near-threshold result: the high EAP-IG$\\times$EP Jaccard (0.582) limits the filtering power of consensus, but ACDC's orthogonality provides just enough independent signal to make consensus informative.\n201\t\n202\t\\paragraph{Factual Recall (GPT-2 Small).} Table~\\ref{tab:factual_detail} shows results for the factual recall task, which serves as a critical interpolation point between IOI ($J_{\\max} = 0.307$) and SVA ($J_{\\max} = 0.582$).\n203\t\n204\t\\begin{table}[t]\n205\t\\centering\n206\t\\caption{Factual Recall circuit discovery results (GPT-2 Small, $k=50$, mean ablation).}\n207\t\\label{tab:factual_detail}\n208\t\\small\n209\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n210\t\\toprule\n211\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR (mean)} & \\textbf{CMD (mean)} \\\\\n212\t\\midrule\n213\tCSS=1.0 & 12 & 1.145 & 0.408 \\\\\n214\tCSS$\\geq$2/3 & 40 & 1.118 & 0.435 \\\\\n215\tACDC & 50 & 1.105 & 0.448 \\\\\n216\tEAP-IG & 50 & 1.098 & 0.455 \\\\\n217\tEP & 50 & 1.085 & 0.468 \\\\\n218\t\\bottomrule\n219\t\\end{tabular}\n220\t\\end{table}\n221\t\n222\tWith $J_{\\max} = 0.48$ and $\\Delta = +0.040$, Factual Recall fills the gap between IOI and the SVA/Arithmetic near-threshold region. The three methods agree on MLP storage edges (layers 5--9) but diverge on attention routing edges, producing moderate Jaccard and moderate but reliable \\css{} advantage.\n223\t\n224\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Generalization}\n225\t\\label{sec:cross_model}\n226\t\n227\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} compares IOI results across all four architectures. \\css{} advantage is consistently positive and of similar magnitude despite substantial architectural differences (standard attention vs.\\ GQA, MLP vs.\\ SwiGLU, 12 to 32 layers).\n228\t\n229\t\\begin{table}[t]\n230\t\\centering\n231\t\\caption{IOI cross-model comparison. Edge\\% = CSS=1.0 edges as fraction of total model edges.}\n232\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n233\t\\small\n234\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n235\t\\toprule\n236\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{$J_{\\max}$} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{Edge\\%} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{Best} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n237\t & & & \\textbf{Edges} & & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tGPT-2 & 117M & 0.307 & 7 & 0.022\\% & 0.109 & 0.025 & +0.084 \\\\\n240\tQwen-2.5 & 0.5B & 0.381 & 62 & 0.035\\% & 0.142 & 0.072 & +0.070 \\\\\n241\tGemma-2 & 2B & 0.288 & 55 & --- & 0.128 & 0.045 & +0.083 \\\\\n242\tLlama-3.1 & 8B & 0.241 & 48 & --- & 0.118 & 0.043 & +0.075 \\\\\n243\t\\bottomrule\n244\t\\end{tabular}\n245\t\\end{table}\n246\t\n247\tA notable trend: $J_{\\max}$ decreases with model size (0.307 $\\to$ 0.241 from GPT-2 to Llama-3.1), reflecting that larger models have more redundant heads for IOI functionality, causing methods to diverge further in their selections. The \\css{} advantage remains stable across this range, suggesting that consensus utility is scale-invariant for low-Jaccard tasks.\n248\t\n249\t\\subsection{Pairwise Jaccard Analysis}\n250\t\\label{sec:jaccard}\n251\t\n252\tTable~\\ref{tab:jaccard_matrices} presents the pairwise Jaccard matrices for representative conditions.\n253\t\n254\t\\begin{table}[t]\n255\t\\centering\n256\t\\caption{Pairwise Jaccard matrices. Left: IOI (GPT-2, $k=50$). Center: SVA (GPT-2, $k=50$). Right: IOI (Qwen, top-500).}\n257\t\\label{tab:jaccard_matrices}\n258\t\\small\n259\t\\begin{tabular}{l|ccc}\n260\t\\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textbf{IOI (GPT-2)}} \\\\\n261\t\\toprule\n262\t & EAP-IG & EP & ACDC \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\tEAP-IG & --- & 0.307 & --- \\\\\n265\tEP & 0.307 & --- & --- \\\\\n266\tACDC & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n267\t\\bottomrule\n268\t\\end{tabular}\n269\t\\quad\n270\t\\begin{tabular}{l|ccc}\n271\t\\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textbf{SVA (GPT-2)}} \\\\\n272\t\\toprule\n273\t & EAP-IG & EP & ACDC \\\\\n274\t\\midrule\n275\tEAP-IG & --- & 0.582 & 0.253 \\\\\n276\tEP & 0.582 & --- & 0.347 \\\\\n277\tACDC & 0.253 & 0.347 & --- \\\\\n278\t\\bottomrule\n279\t\\end{tabular}\n280\t\\quad\n281\t\\begin{tabular}{l|ccc}\n282\t\\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textbf{IOI (Qwen)}} \\\\\n283\t\\toprule\n284\t & EAP-IG & EP & ACDC \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tEAP-IG & --- & 0.381 & 0.142 \\\\\n287\tEP & 0.381 & --- & 0.113 \\\\\n288\tACDC & 0.142 & 0.113 & --- \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\tA consistent structural pattern emerges: EAP-IG$\\times$EP Jaccard (both gradient-adjacent) is always the highest pair, while ACDC$\\times$gradient Jaccard is substantially lower. On Qwen IOI, ACDC$\\times$EAP-IG = 0.142 and ACDC$\\times$EP = 0.113, confirming that activation patching provides genuinely orthogonal evidence from gradient methods. This motivates the three-family design: even when two methods are moderately correlated, a sufficiently orthogonal third method enables informative consensus.\n294\t\n295\t\\subsection{Negative Results: Arithmetic}\n296\t\\label{sec:arithmetic}\n297\t\n298\tTable~\\ref{tab:arithmetic} presents the Arithmetic results, which serve as predicted negative controls.\n299\t\n300\t\\begin{table}[t]\n301\t\\centering\n302\t\\caption{Arithmetic cross-model comparison (mean ablation). CSS fails on both models as predicted by high $J_{\\max}$.}\n303\t\\label{tab:arithmetic}\n304\t\\small\n305\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n306\t\\toprule\n307\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{$J_{\\max}$} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{Best Single} & \\textbf{Best Single} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n308\t & & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\\\\n309\t\\midrule\n310\tGPT-2 & 0.695 & --- & --- & --- & --- & $-0.066$ \\\\\n311\tQwen-2.5 & 0.553 & 85 & 0.960 & EAP-IG & 0.985 & $-0.025$ \\\\\n312\t\\bottomrule\n313\t\\end{tabular}\n314\t\\end{table}\n315\t\n316\tArithmetic's computation structure---comparison heads and magnitude-encoding MLPs---forces all performance-based methods to converge on the same bottleneck edges. On Qwen, ACDC's replacement of a degenerate method (IFR) reduces $J_{\\max}$ from 0.695 to 0.553, but the task-structural convergence persists: $\\Delta$ improves from $-0.066$ to $-0.025$ but remains negative. This confirms that \\css{} utility is fundamentally limited by task structure, not just method choice.\n317\t\n318\t\\subsection{Jaccard--Utility Correlation (Claim 2)}\n319\t\\label{sec:correlation}\n320\t\n321\tTable~\\ref{tab:correlation} and the associated analysis constitute the central quantitative finding. Across all 6 model$\\times$task data points, we observe a strong negative correlation between $J_{\\max}$ and \\css{} CPR advantage.\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{table}[t]\n324\t\\centering\n325\t\\caption{Jaccard--CSS utility correlation across all conditions. Spearman $\\rho = 0.943$, $p = 0.005$. Fitted threshold: $J_{\\max} \\approx 0.55$ [95\\% CI: 0.50--0.60].}\n326\t\\label{tab:correlation}\n327\t\\small\n328\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n329\t\\toprule\n330\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{$J_{\\max}$} & \\textbf{$1 - J_{\\max}$} & \\textbf{CSS $\\Delta$} & \\textbf{PASS} & \\textbf{Div.\\ Rank} \\\\\n331\t\\midrule\n332\tIOI & GPT-2 & 0.307 & 0.693 & +0.080 & \\checkmark & 1 \\\\\n333\tIOI & Qwen-2.5 & 0.381 & 0.619 & +0.070 & \\checkmark & 2 \\\\\n334\tFactual & GPT-2 & 0.480 & 0.520 & +0.040 & \\checkmark & 3 \\\\\n335\tArithmetic & Qwen-2.5 & 0.553 & 0.447 & $-0.025$ & $\\times$ & 4 \\\\\n336\tSVA & GPT-2 & 0.582 & 0.418 & +0.035 & \\checkmark & 5 \\\\\n337\tArithmetic & GPT-2 & 0.695 & 0.305 & $-0.066$ & $\\times$ & 6 \\\\\n338\t\\bottomrule\n339\t\\end{tabular}\n340\t\\end{table}\n341\t\n342\tThe rank correlation is near-perfect: diversity rank and advantage rank agree on 5 of 6 positions, with the only swap occurring between Qwen Arithmetic (diversity rank 4, advantage rank 5) and SVA (diversity rank 5, advantage rank 4). These two cases have similar $J_{\\max}$ (0.553 vs.\\ 0.582) but opposite $\\Delta$ signs, reflecting the near-threshold ambiguity.\n343\t\n344\tSVA's anomalous positive result at $J_{\\max} = 0.582$ (above the fitted 0.55 threshold) is explained by examining the full Jaccard structure: while EAP-IG$\\times$EP is high (0.582), ACDC$\\times$EAP-IG (0.253) and ACDC$\\times$EP (0.347) are low. The \\emph{effective} diversity is higher than $J_{\\max}$ alone suggests, because one method (ACDC) is sufficiently orthogonal to compensate for the correlation between the other two.\n345\t\n346\tWithin GPT-2 alone (4 tasks), the rank correlation is perfect ($\\rho = 1.0$, $p = 0.042$), demonstrating that the relationship holds even controlling for model architecture.\n347\t\n348\tLow-Jaccard tasks ($J_{\\max} < 0.55$) show a 3/3 (100\\%) \\css{} PASS rate. Among tasks near or above the threshold, the PASS rate drops to 1/3 (SVA being the anomalous positive case discussed above).\n349\t\n350\t% ============================================================\n351\t\\section{Analysis and Ablations}\n352\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n353\t% ============================================================\n354\t\n355\t\\subsection{Binarization Sensitivity}\n356\t\\label{sec:sensitivity}\n357\t\n358\tA natural concern is whether \\css{} results depend critically on the binarization threshold $k$. Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity} presents a systematic sweep over $k \\in \\{50, 75, 100, 150\\}$ on IOI and SVA.\n359\t\n360\t\\begin{table}[t]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\caption{Binarization sensitivity. IOI evaluated under zero ablation; SVA under mean ablation.}\n363\t\\label{tab:sensitivity}\n364\t\\small\n365\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n366\t\\toprule\n367\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{$k$} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0} & \\textbf{Best Single} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n368\t & & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\\\\n369\t\\midrule\n370\t\\multirow{4}{*}{IOI} & 50 & 7 & 0.109 & 0.025 & +0.084 \\\\\n371\t & 75 & 12 & 0.102 & 0.023 & +0.079 \\\\\n372\t & 100 & 18 & 0.098 & 0.022 & +0.076 \\\\\n373\t & 150 & 24 & 0.091 & 0.020 & +0.071 \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\t\\multirow{4}{*}{SVA} & 50 & 14 & 1.210 & 1.175 & +0.035 \\\\\n376\t & 75 & 22 & 1.198 & 1.177 & +0.021 \\\\\n377\t & 100 & 30 & 1.190 & 1.178 & +0.012 \\\\\n378\t & 150 & 40 & 1.178 & 1.179 & $-0.001$ \\\\\n379\t\\bottomrule\n380\t\\end{tabular}\n381\t\\end{table}\n382\t\n383\tIOI's \\css{} advantage is \\textbf{$k$-robust}: $\\Delta$ remains strongly positive across all tested values (0.071--0.084), with maximum CPR variation of only 0.018. This reflects IOI's low $J_{\\max}$: consensus is informative regardless of the exact binarization point.\n384\t\n385\tSVA's advantage is \\textbf{$k$-sensitive}: $\\Delta$ is positive only at $k=50$ (+0.035) and vanishes by $k=150$ ($-0.001$). As $k$ increases, pairwise overlap grows, and the near-threshold SVA case loses its marginal consensus signal. This confirms the Jaccard--utility relationship at a finer granularity: $k$-robustness itself is predicted by $J_{\\max}$.\n386\t\n387\t\\subsection{Ablation Method Robustness}\n388\t\\label{sec:ablation_comparison}\n389\t\n390\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_methods} compares \\css{} advantage under three ablation evaluation methods.\n391\t\n392\t\\begin{table}[t]\n393\t\\centering\n394\t\\caption{CSS advantage under different ablation methods. Resample uses $n=10$ random seeds; mean $\\pm$ std reported.}\n395\t\\label{tab:ablation_methods}\n396\t\\small\n397\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n398\t\\toprule\n399\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Ablation} & \\textbf{CSS=1.0 CPR} & \\textbf{Best Single CPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n400\t\\midrule\n401\t\\multirow{3}{*}{IOI} & Zero & 0.109 & 0.029 & +0.080 \\\\\n402\t & Mean & 0.145 & 0.065 & +0.080 \\\\\n403\t & Resample & $0.125 \\pm 0.018$ & $0.045 \\pm 0.012$ & $+0.080 \\pm 0.012$ \\\\\n404\t\\midrule\n405\t\\multirow{3}{*}{SVA} & Zero & $-0.482$ & $-0.371$ & $-0.111$ \\\\\n406\t & Mean & 1.210 & 1.175 & +0.035 \\\\\n407\t & Resample & $1.155 \\pm 0.032$ & $1.125 \\pm 0.015$ & $+0.030 \\pm 0.015$ \\\\\n408\t\\bottomrule\n409\t\\end{tabular}\n410\t\\end{table}\n411\t\n412\tIOI's advantage is \\textbf{perfectly stable} across all three methods ($\\Delta = +0.080$), ruling out evaluation artifacts. SVA shows ablation-method dependence: zero ablation yields $\\Delta = -0.111$ (the 14 highly localized consensus edges suffer cascading destruction under zero ablation), while mean and resample ablation yield positive $\\Delta$. This is not a weakness of \\css{} but a known property of evaluation methodology: zero ablation is overly destructive for small, localized edge sets.\n413\t\n414\t\\subsection{Comparison with CIRCUS}\n415\t\\label{sec:circus}\n416\t\n417\tCIRCUS \\citep{parekh2026circus} identifies ``core'' circuit edges by sweeping thresholds within a single method (EAP-IG) and retaining edges stable across thresholds. This captures within-method robustness but cannot correct method-specific systematic biases. Table~\\ref{tab:circus} presents a head-to-head comparison.\n418\t\n419\t\\begin{table}[t]\n420\t\\centering\n421\t\\caption{CIRCUS vs.\\ \\css{} head-to-head comparison. IOI evaluated under zero ablation; SVA and Factual under mean ablation.}\n422\t\\label{tab:circus}\n423\t\\small\n424\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n425\t\\toprule\n426\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Edges} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$\\,(CSS$-$CIRCUS)} \\\\\n427\t\\midrule\n428\t\\multirow{2}{*}{IOI} & CIRCUS core & 15 & $-0.108$ & \\multirow{2}{*}{+0.217} \\\\\n429\t & CSS=1.0 & 7 & 0.109 & \\\\\n430\t\\midrule\n431\t\\multirow{2}{*}{SVA} & CIRCUS core & 12 & 0.994 & \\multirow{2}{*}{+0.216} \\\\\n432\t & CSS=1.0 & 14 & 1.210 & \\\\\n433\t\\midrule\n434\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Factual} & CIRCUS core & 16 & 0.982 & \\multirow{2}{*}{+0.163} \\\\\n435\t & CSS=1.0 & 12 & 1.145 & \\\\\n436\t\\bottomrule\n437\t\\end{tabular}\n438\t\\end{table}\n439\t\n440\t\\css{} outperforms CIRCUS on all three CSS-positive tasks. The advantage is largest on IOI (+0.217), where CIRCUS's EAP-IG ``core'' edges actually have \\emph{negative} CPR under zero ablation ($-0.108$). These edges are gradient artifacts---high and stable EAP-IG scores that do not correspond to functional circuit components. Cross-method consensus naturally filters such artifacts because ACDC and EP do not select them. On SVA (+0.216) and Factual Recall (+0.163), CIRCUS identifies functional edges but misses the additional filtering that cross-method agreement provides.\n441\t\n442\t\\subsection{IFR Method Divergence}\n443\t\\label{sec:ifr}\n444\t\n445\tDuring cross-model validation, we discovered that IFR (Intermediate Feature Representation similarity) completely degenerates on Qwen-2.5's GQA architecture. Table~\\ref{tab:ifr} documents this failure.\n446\t\n447\t\\begin{table}[t]\n448\t\\centering\n449\t\\caption{IFR vs.\\ ACDC as third method on Qwen-2.5 IOI. IFR degenerates under GQA architecture.}\n450\t\\label{tab:ifr}\n451\t\\small\n452\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccl}\n453\t\\toprule\n454\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{$J$(EAP-IG)} & \\textbf{$J$(EP)} & \\textbf{CPR} & \\textbf{Layer Focus} \\\\\n455\t\\midrule\n456\tIFR & 0.000 & 0.000 & $\\sim$0.0 & Layers 0--5 (early MLP) \\\\\n457\tACDC & 0.142 & 0.113 & 0.072 & Layers 8--22 (dispersed) \\\\\n458\t\\bottomrule\n459\t\\end{tabular}\n460\t\\end{table}\n461\t\n462\tIFR uses intermediate representation cosine similarity to score edges. Under GQA, key-value sharing across attention heads absorbs single-edge representation changes in deep layers, causing IFR to select only early-layer MLP edges with large but functionally unimportant representation effects. The result is zero overlap with gradient methods and zero CPR---a complete failure. ACDC, which measures logit-level effects of clean$\\to$corrupt activation substitution, is unaffected by GQA's representation absorption. This finding has practical implications: method family selection for \\css{} must account for architectural compatibility, and we recommend EAP-IG + EP + ACDC as a robust default for modern architectures ($\\geq$0.5B parameters).\n463\t\n464\t% ============================================================\n465\t\\section{Discussion}\n466\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n467\t% ============================================================\n468\t\n469\t\\paragraph{Effective diversity vs.\\ maximum pairwise Jaccard.} Our fitted threshold of $J_{\\max} \\approx 0.55$ is an imperfect predictor: SVA ($J_{\\max} = 0.582$) is a positive case despite exceeding the threshold. Examining the full Jaccard structure reveals that ACDC's low correlation with both gradient methods (0.253, 0.347) partially compensates for the high EAP-IG$\\times$EP correlation. A more refined diversity metric---perhaps the harmonic mean or minimum of all pairwise Jaccards---could improve prediction. We leave this to future work.\n470\t\n471\t\\paragraph{Ablation method selection.} Our results highlight that evaluation methodology interacts with circuit locality. Zero ablation is appropriate for distributed circuits but overly destructive for small, localized consensus cores (as in SVA). We recommend mean ablation as the default for \\css{} evaluation, with zero ablation as a robustness check for non-localized tasks.\n472\t\n473\t\\paragraph{Scalability.} \\css{} requires running $K$ independent discovery methods, which multiplies computational cost. However, the methods need not be run to convergence---approximate top-$k$ rankings suffice. Additionally, the Jaccard diagnostic can be computed before the full evaluation pipeline, enabling early termination when $J_{\\max}$ exceeds the threshold.\n474\t\n475\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} With 6 data points, the Jaccard--utility correlation, while statistically significant ($p = 0.005$), should be validated on additional tasks and models. The fitted threshold ($0.55 \\pm 0.05$) may shift with different method combinations or evaluation protocols. Our method family recommendation (EAP-IG + EP + ACDC) has not been tested on all architectures beyond GQA, SwiGLU, and sliding-window attention.\n476\t\n477\t% ============================================================\n478\t\\section{Related Work}\n479\t\\label{sec:related}\n480\t% ============================================================\n481\t\n482\t\\paragraph{Circuit discovery methods.} Automated circuit discovery has been approached through activation patching \\citep{conmy2023automated}, gradient attribution \\citep{syed2023attribution}, edge pruning \\citep{hanna2024have}, and subnetwork probing \\citep{cao2021low}. Each method embodies different assumptions about what constitutes a ``circuit edge,'' leading to systematic disagreements \\citep{mondorf2024beyond}. \\css{} exploits these disagreements rather than treating them as a problem.\n483\t\n484\t\\paragraph{Cross-method comparison.} \\citet{mondorf2024beyond} compare circuit discovery methods using score averaging within the gradient family. Our approach differs in two respects: we use binary consensus (threshold-free) rather than score averaging (requires calibration), and we explicitly include methods from different algorithmic families (gradient, pruning, activation patching) rather than restricting to gradient variants.\n485\t\n486\t\\paragraph{Within-method robustness.} CIRCUS \\citep{parekh2026circus} identifies stable edges by sweeping binarization thresholds within a single method. This captures threshold robustness but not cross-method validity. Our comparison (Section~\\ref{sec:circus}) shows that within-method stability can amplify systematic biases rather than correcting them.\n487\t\n488\t\\paragraph{Ensemble methods in interpretability.} PIE \\citep{zhang2024pie} uses iterative pruning ensembles but operates within a single algorithmic paradigm. \\css{} is distinguished by requiring methods from distinct families, providing diversity guarantees that within-paradigm ensembles cannot offer.\n489\t\n490\t% ============================================================\n491\t\\section{Conclusion}\n492\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n493\t% ============================================================\n494\t\n495\tWe introduced Consensus Circuit Scoring (\\css{}), a simple binary consensus framework for identifying faithful circuit subsets from multiple discovery methods. Our experiments across 4 tasks and 4 model architectures (117M--8B parameters) establish two main findings: (1) \\cssone{} circuits are dramatically smaller yet more faithful than single-method circuits, but only under sufficient method diversity ($J_{\\max} < 0.55$); and (2) inter-method Jaccard is a strong predictor of consensus utility ($\\rho = 0.943$, $p = 0.005$), providing a practical diagnostic for when consensus-based identification is worthwhile. We further show that \\css{} outperforms within-method robustness approaches (CIRCUS) and is robust across evaluation methods and binarization thresholds for low-Jaccard tasks. These results suggest that method disagreement in circuit discovery is not merely noise but contains actionable signal about circuit structure.\n496\t\n497\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n498\t\\bibliography{references}\n499\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013XjSyj7kThM1sVp2qFHJq9","session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"a5f0b717-ea8e-4138-a047-7d8d5006c32e","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:06:39.078Z"}
