{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C9APy7MBjWDXtXUoe6u1sG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n13\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\css}{\\textsc{CSS}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\circus}{\\textsc{Circus}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\eapig}{\\textsc{EAP-IG}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\ep}{\\textsc{EP}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\acdc}{\\textsc{ACDC}}\n21\t\\newcommand{\\cpr}{\\text{CPR}}\n22\t\\newcommand{\\cmd}{\\text{CMD}}\n23\t\n24\t\\title{Why Cross-Method Circuit Consensus Fails:\\\\An Empirical Analysis}\n25\t\n26\t\\author{Anonymous}\n27\t\n28\t\\begin{document}\n29\t\\maketitle\n30\t\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tCircuit discovery methods---such as Edge Attribution Patching (\\eapig{}), Edge Pruning (\\ep{}), and Automated Circuit Discovery (\\acdc{})---aim to identify minimal subgraphs that faithfully explain model behavior. A natural hypothesis is that \\emph{consensus} across independently derived circuits should yield higher-quality, more faithful subgraphs.\n34\tWe systematically evaluate this hypothesis through \\emph{Cross-method Structural consensus Scoring} (\\css{}), a binary consensus framework applied to $K \\ge 3$ circuit discovery methods across multiple tasks and models.\n35\tOur findings are predominantly negative.\n36\tOn GPT-2 Small, \\css{} produces a faithful consensus circuit only on Indirect Object Identification (IOI), while failing on Subject--Verb Agreement (SVA) and Arithmetic---a 1/3 success rate.\n37\tMoreover, the sole positive result is confounded by two evaluation artifacts: \\acdc{}'s \\texttt{isinf} binarization and zero-ablation size bias.\n38\tOn Qwen-2.5 0.5B (179k edges), all methods exhibit near-zero pairwise overlap (Jaccard $\\approx 0.04$), rendering consensus trivially empty.\n39\tWe identify three structural causes of failure: (1) high inter-method correlation on most tasks eliminates the independent-evidence premise of consensus; (2) \\acdc{} binarization pathologies inflate apparent method diversity; and (3) combinatorial dilution in larger edge spaces systematically destroys cross-method overlap.\n40\tThese results suggest that binary circuit consensus is viable only within a narrow ``Goldilocks zone'' of inter-method agreement, which rarely obtains in practice.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t% ============================================================\n44\t\\section{Introduction}\n45\t\\label{sec:intro}\n46\t\n47\tMechanistic interpretability aims to understand neural network computation by identifying \\emph{circuits}---subgraphs of the computational graph that are necessary and sufficient for a given behavior \\citep{olah2020zoom,wang2023interpretability,conmy2023automated}.\n48\tMultiple circuit discovery methods have been proposed, including gradient-based attribution \\citep{syed2023attribution}, edge pruning \\citep{hanna2024does}, and automated search \\citep{conmy2023automated}.\n49\tA persistent challenge is that different methods often identify substantially different circuits for the same task \\citep{mondorf2025beyond}, raising the question: which edges are ``real''?\n50\t\n51\tOne intuitive approach is \\emph{consensus}: edges identified by multiple independent methods may constitute a more reliable circuit.\n52\tThis idea has been explored through score averaging \\citep{mondorf2025beyond} and within-method threshold diversity \\citep[\\circus{};][]{parekh2026circus}, but systematic cross-method binary consensus remains understudied.\n53\t\n54\tWe introduce \\emph{Cross-method Structural consensus Scoring} (\\css{}), which assigns each edge a score $s \\in \\{1/K, 2/K, \\ldots, 1\\}$ based on the fraction of $K$ methods that include it.\n55\tThe \\css{}$= 1.0$ core---edges selected by all methods---is hypothesized to be maximally faithful and edge-efficient.\n56\t\n57\tOur extensive experiments reveal that this hypothesis \\textbf{fails in the majority of settings}.\n58\tAcross three tasks on GPT-2 Small and two tasks on Qwen-2.5 0.5B, \\css{} produces a positive result only for IOI on GPT-2, and even this case is confounded by evaluation artifacts.\n59\tRather than presenting \\css{} as a method, we pivot to analyzing \\emph{why} consensus fails, yielding three contributions:\n60\t\n61\t\\begin{enumerate}\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Method correlation undermines consensus.} On SVA and Arithmetic, gradient-based methods (\\eapig{}, \\ep{}) exhibit high pairwise Jaccard ($\\ge 0.65$), violating the independent-evidence assumption. The resulting consensus adds no information beyond the most selective single method (\\S\\ref{sec:gpt2}).\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{Binarization pathologies inflate diversity.} \\acdc{}'s default \\texttt{isinf} threshold produces extremely sparse circuits (14--17 edges vs.\\ 100 for gradient methods), artificially lowering Jaccard and inflating apparent consensus value. Top-$k$ normalization partially corrects this but does not rescue consensus on failing tasks (\\S\\ref{sec:binarization}).\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{Combinatorial dilution destroys consensus at scale.} On Qwen-2.5 0.5B (179k edges), the same top-0.3\\% binarization that works on GPT-2 (32k edges) yields near-zero Jaccard ($\\le 0.042$) and an empty \\css{}$= 1.0$ set, demonstrating that edge-level consensus does not scale (\\S\\ref{sec:scaling}).\n65\t\\end{enumerate}\n66\t\n67\t% ============================================================\n68\t\\section{Background and Method}\n69\t\\label{sec:method}\n70\t\n71\t\\subsection{Circuit Discovery Methods}\n72\t\n73\tWe employ three methods from different algorithmic families:\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{\\eapig{} (Edge Attribution Patching with Integrated Gradients)} computes edge importance via the integrated gradient of the patching effect along an interpolation path between clean and corrupted activations \\citep{syed2023attribution}.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{\\ep{} (Edge Pruning)} learns a continuous mask over edges, optimizing for faithfulness while encouraging sparsity through $L_1$ regularization \\citep{hanna2024does}.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{\\acdc{} (Automated Circuit Discovery)} iteratively prunes edges by testing whether each edge's removal changes model output beyond a threshold \\citep{conmy2023automated}.\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Cross-method Structural Consensus Scoring}\n82\t\n83\tGiven $K$ circuit discovery methods, each producing a binary edge set $C_k \\subseteq E$ over the full edge set $E$ of a transformer, we define:\n84\t\\begin{equation}\n85\t    \\css{}(e) = \\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\mathbf{1}[e \\in C_k]\n86\t\\end{equation}\n87\tThe \\css{}$= 1.0$ core is $\\{e : \\css{}(e) = 1\\}$, containing edges selected by all $K$ methods.\n88\t\n89\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n90\t\n91\tWe evaluate circuits using:\n92\t\\begin{itemize}\n93\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit Performance Recovery (CPR)}: the fraction of clean-model performance recovered by the circuit. $\\cpr = 1.0$ means perfect recovery.\n94\t    \\item \\textbf{Circuit Minimality Deviation (CMD)}: a measure of circuit size relative to the full graph. Lower is better.\n95\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise Jaccard}: $J(C_i, C_j) = |C_i \\cap C_j| / |C_i \\cup C_j|$, measuring overlap between two method outputs.\n96\t\\end{itemize}\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Binarization}\n99\t\\label{sec:binarization_method}\n100\t\n101\tA critical preprocessing step is converting continuous scores (\\eapig{}, \\ep{}) or variable-size outputs (\\acdc{}) into comparable binary edge sets.\n102\tWe use \\textbf{top-$k$} binarization: select the top $k$ edges by importance score for each method.\n103\tWe also examine \\acdc{}'s default \\textbf{\\texttt{isinf}} binarization, which retains only edges with infinite importance (those whose removal changes the output token), typically yielding 14--17 edges.\n104\t\n105\t% ============================================================\n106\t\\section{GPT-2 Small Experiments}\n107\t\\label{sec:gpt2}\n108\t\n109\tWe evaluate \\css{} with $K = 3$ (\\eapig{}, \\ep{}, \\acdc{}) on three tasks using GPT-2 Small (117M parameters, 32,491 total edges).\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Indirect Object Identification (IOI)}\n112\t\n113\tIOI requires the model to predict the indirect object in sentences like ``When Mary and John went to the store, John gave a drink to \\underline{\\hspace{1cm}}''.\n114\t\n115\t\\paragraph{Results.} With top-$k$ binarization ($k = 100$), pairwise Jaccard values are moderate: $J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\ep{}}) = 0.307$, $J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\acdc{}}) = 0.093$, $J(\\text{\\ep{}}, \\text{\\acdc{}}) = 0.133$.\n116\tThe \\css{}$= 1.0$ core contains 7 edges with CPR $= 0.109$ under zero ablation, exceeding all single-method baselines at $k = 75$ (best single CPR $= 0.029$).\n117\tThis is the \\textbf{only positive result} across all experiments.\n118\t\n119\t\\subsection{Subject--Verb Agreement (SVA)}\n120\t\n121\tSVA requires predicting the correct verb number in sentences with intervening attractors.\n122\t\n123\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n124\tThe gradient methods are highly correlated: $J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\ep{}}) = 0.653$, near the failure threshold.\n125\tThe \\css{}$= 1.0$ core contains 44 edges with mean CPR $= 1.173$, while the best single method (\\acdc{} at $k = 75$) achieves CPR $= 1.175$.\n126\tMulti-seed bootstrap analysis ($n = 5$ seeds) yields $\\Delta\\text{CPR} = 0.000 \\pm 0.009$ with 95\\% CI $[-0.012, +0.019]$ spanning zero.\n127\tTable~\\ref{tab:sva_bootstrap} shows per-seed results.\n128\t\n129\t\\begin{table}[t]\n130\t\\centering\n131\t\\small\n132\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n133\t\\toprule\n134\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{\\css{}$=1.0$ CPR} & \\textbf{\\acdc{} CPR} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\\\\n135\t\\midrule\n136\t42 (original)   & 1.187 & 1.175 & $+0.013$ \\\\\n137\t123             & 1.168 & 1.179 & $-0.011$ \\\\\n138\t456             & 1.178 & 1.182 & $-0.004$ \\\\\n139\t789             & 1.175 & 1.170 & $+0.005$ \\\\\n140\t1024            & 1.171 & 1.174 & $-0.003$ \\\\\n141\t\\midrule\n142\tMean $\\pm$ std  & $1.176 \\pm 0.007$ & $1.176 \\pm 0.005$ & $0.000 \\pm 0.009$ \\\\\n143\t\\bottomrule\n144\t\\end{tabular}\n145\t\\caption{SVA multi-seed bootstrap analysis. The apparent advantage at seed 42 vanishes under replication; the 95\\% CI for $\\Delta$CPR includes zero.}\n146\t\\label{tab:sva_bootstrap}\n147\t\\end{table}\n148\t\n149\t\\begin{table}[t]\n150\t\\centering\n151\t\\small\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Result} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\t$J_{\\max} < 0.70$ & PASS (0.653) & marginal \\\\\n157\t$|\\css{} \\ge 2/3| \\ge 10$ & PASS (54) & \\\\\n158\t$|\\css{} = 1.0| \\ge 3$ & PASS (44) & \\\\\n159\tCPR dominance & FAIL ($\\Delta \\approx 0$) & CI includes zero \\\\\n160\t\\bottomrule\n161\t\\end{tabular}\n162\t\\caption{SVA diagnostic conditions. The high inter-method Jaccard (0.653) and absence of CPR dominance indicate consensus failure.}\n163\t\\label{tab:sva_conditions}\n164\t\\end{table}\n165\t\n166\t\\paragraph{Verdict: FAIL.} The high $J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\ep{}})$ means gradient methods dominate the consensus, and \\css{} reduces to a noisy version of \\acdc{}.\n167\t\n168\t\\subsection{Arithmetic (Two-Digit Addition)}\n169\t\n170\tThe model must produce the correct sum for prompts like ``47 + 38 =''.\n171\t\n172\t\\paragraph{Results.}\n173\tInter-method correlation is the highest across all tasks: $J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\ep{}}) = 0.695$.\n174\tUnder mean ablation, \\css{}$= 1.0$ achieves CPR $= 0.919$ vs.\\ best single method CPR $= 0.985$, a \\emph{deficit} of $-0.066$.\n175\t\n176\t\\paragraph{Verdict: FAIL.} When methods substantially agree, consensus merely selects the intersection---which is smaller but not more faithful.\n177\t\n178\t\\subsection{Factual Recall (Abandoned)}\n179\t\n180\tWe attempted factual recall (country--capital pairs) on GPT-2 Small but abandoned the experiment due to task--model mismatch: baseline accuracy was only 52\\% ($\\pm 15\\%$), barely above the 25\\% random baseline.\n181\t\\acdc{} could not converge under such unstable baselines, and gradient methods produced circuits with near-zero faithfulness (\\eapig{} $k = 50$: CPR $= 0.02$; \\ep{} $k = 50$: CPR $= -0.01$).\n182\tGPT-2 Small lacks sufficient parametric knowledge for localized factual retrieval circuits.\n183\t\n184\t\\subsection{GPT-2 Summary}\n185\t\n186\tTable~\\ref{tab:gpt2_summary} summarizes all GPT-2 results. \\css{} succeeds only on IOI (1/3 tasks), where max pairwise Jaccard is lowest (0.307).\n187\t\n188\t\\begin{table}[t]\n189\t\\centering\n190\t\\small\n191\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n192\t\\toprule\n193\t\\textbf{Task} & $\\boldsymbol{J_{\\max}}$ & \\textbf{\\css{}$=1.0$ CPR} & \\textbf{Best Single} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n194\t\\midrule\n195\tIOI        & 0.307 & $+0.109$ & $+0.029$ & PASS \\\\\n196\tSVA        & 0.653 & $+1.173$ & $+1.175$ & FAIL \\\\\n197\tArithmetic & 0.695 & $+0.919$ & $+0.985$ & FAIL \\\\\n198\t\\bottomrule\n199\t\\end{tabular}\n200\t\\caption{GPT-2 Small results across three tasks. \\css{} shows advantage only on IOI, where inter-method Jaccard is lowest. CPR values for IOI use zero ablation; SVA and Arithmetic use mean ablation.}\n201\t\\label{tab:gpt2_summary}\n202\t\\end{table}\n203\t\n204\t% ============================================================\n205\t\\section{Binarization and Ablation Confounds}\n206\t\\label{sec:binarization}\n207\t\n208\tThe sole positive result (IOI) is subject to two confounds that we analyze in detail.\n209\t\n210\t\\subsection{\\acdc{} \\texttt{isinf} vs.\\ Top-$k$ Binarization}\n211\t\n212\t\\acdc{}'s default \\texttt{isinf} threshold retains only 14 edges on IOI, compared to 100 for gradient methods at $k = 100$.\n213\tThis extreme sparsity mismatch artificially deflates $J(\\text{\\acdc{}}, \\cdot)$ and makes \\css{}$= 1.0 \\approx$ \\acdc{}.\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[t]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\small\n218\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n219\t\\toprule\n220\t\\textbf{Task} & $\\boldsymbol{J}$ \\textbf{(isinf)} & $\\boldsymbol{J}$ \\textbf{(top-$k$)} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta J}$ & \\textbf{Outcome change?} \\\\\n221\t\\midrule\n222\tIOI        & 0.09 & 0.25 & $+0.16$ & No (still PASS) \\\\\n223\tArithmetic & 0.17 & 0.42 & $+0.25$ & No (still FAIL) \\\\\n224\tSVA        & 0.37 & 0.37 & $0$ & N/A (already top-$k$) \\\\\n225\t\\bottomrule\n226\t\\end{tabular}\n227\t\\caption{Effect of \\acdc{} binarization on Jaccard. \\texttt{isinf} systematically deflates Jaccard by producing extremely sparse circuits.}\n228\t\\label{tab:acdc_binarization}\n229\t\\end{table}\n230\t\n231\tTop-$k$ normalization raises IOI's $J(\\text{\\acdc{}}, \\cdot)$ from 0.09 to 0.25 (Table~\\ref{tab:acdc_binarization}), but the \\css{} positive result persists---indicating that while \\texttt{isinf} inflates apparent diversity, it is not the sole driver.\n232\t\n233\t\\subsection{Top-$k$ Sensitivity Sweep}\n234\t\n235\tWe sweep $k \\in \\{50, 75, 100, 150\\}$ across all GPT-2 tasks (Table~\\ref{tab:topk_sweep}).\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[t]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\small\n240\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n241\t\\toprule\n242\t\\textbf{Task} & $k{=}50$ & $k{=}75$ & $k{=}100$ & $k{=}150$ & \\textbf{Stable?} & \\textbf{Beats single?} \\\\\n243\t\\midrule\n244\tIOI (zero)     & 0.109 & 0.102 & 0.095 & 0.088 & Yes & Yes (all $k$) \\\\\n245\tSVA (mean)     & 1.185 & 1.187 & 1.174 & 1.168 & Yes & No (all $k$) \\\\\n246\tArith (mean) & 0.919 & 0.919 & 0.926 & 0.933 & Yes & No (all $k$) \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\caption{\\css{}$= 1.0$ CPR across top-$k$ values. \\css{} is stable across $k$ ($\\text{std} < 0.02$), but the failure pattern on SVA and Arithmetic is also stable---binarization is not the cause of failure.}\n250\t\\label{tab:topk_sweep}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013XjSyj7kThM1sVp2qFHJq9","session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"e67e12dc-d08a-4379-8553-cfa930790e1e","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:06:46.076Z"}
