{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DZrUBSfGcRmPwrUwBvgt95","type":"tool_result","content":"250\t\\label{tab:topk_sweep}\n251\t\\end{table}\n252\t\n253\t\\css{} CPR is stable across $k$ (std $< 0.02$ within each task), confirming that binarization threshold is not responsible for consensus failure. The failure is structural: method correlation, not $k$-sensitivity.\n254\t\n255\t\\subsection{Ablation Method Sensitivity}\n256\t\\label{sec:ablation_confound}\n257\t\n258\tWe compare zero, mean, and resample ablation on all three GPT-2 tasks (Table~\\ref{tab:ablation}).\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[t]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\small\n263\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n264\t\\toprule\n265\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Zero} $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ & \\textbf{Mean} $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ & \\textbf{Resample} $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\\\\n266\t\\midrule\n267\tIOI        & $+0.080$ & $+0.007$ & $+0.003$ \\\\\n268\tSVA        & $-0.470$ & $-0.002$ & $-0.020$ \\\\\n269\tArithmetic & $-0.053$ & $-0.066$ & $-0.070$ \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\caption{\\css{} advantage ($\\Delta = \\text{CSS}_{1.0}\\text{ CPR} - \\text{best single CPR}$) by ablation method. IOI's advantage shrinks from $+0.080$ (zero) to $+0.003$ (resample), entering the noise floor.}\n273\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n274\t\\end{table}\n275\t\n276\tIOI's \\css{} advantage drops from $+0.080$ under zero ablation to $+0.007$ (mean) and $+0.003$ (resample).\n277\tZero ablation disproportionately penalizes larger circuits: ablating 93 non-consensus edges to zero causes cascading disruption, while the 7-edge \\css{}$= 1.0$ core suffers minimal cascade loss.\n278\tUnder mean or resample ablation, which mitigate this size bias, the advantage is within noise.\n279\t\n280\tSVA and Arithmetic show negative $\\Delta$ under all ablation methods, confirming their failure is robust.\n281\t\n282\t\\paragraph{Implication.} IOI's positive result requires the conjunction of \\acdc{} \\texttt{isinf} sparsity \\emph{and} zero ablation bias. Removing either confound substantially reduces or eliminates the advantage.\n283\t\n284\t% ============================================================\n285\t\\section{Cross-Model Scaling Failure}\n286\t\\label{sec:scaling}\n287\t\n288\t\\subsection{Qwen-2.5 0.5B: IOI}\n289\t\n290\tTo test generalization, we apply \\css{} to Qwen-2.5 0.5B on IOI.\n291\tThe model has 179,388 total edges---5.5$\\times$ larger than GPT-2 Small's 32,491.\n292\t\n293\tAt top-0.3\\% binarization ($\\sim$538 edges per method), pairwise Jaccard values collapse:\n294\t$J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\ep{}}) = 0.042$,\n295\t$J(\\text{\\eapig{}}, \\text{\\acdc{}}) = 0.006$,\n296\t$J(\\text{\\ep{}}, \\text{\\acdc{}}) = 0.008$.\n297\tThe \\css{}$= 1.0$ core is \\textbf{empty} (0 edges).\n298\tEven the relaxed \\css{}$\\ge 2/3$ set contains only 66 edges with CPR $= 0.031$, far below single-method baselines (best: \\eapig{} CPR $= 0.055$).\n299\t\n300\t\\begin{table}[t]\n301\t\\centering\n302\t\\small\n303\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n304\t\\toprule\n305\t & \\textbf{GPT-2 Small} & \\textbf{Qwen-2.5 0.5B} & \\textbf{Ratio} \\\\\n306\t\\midrule\n307\tTotal edges     & 32,491  & 179,388 & 5.5$\\times$ \\\\\n308\tTop-$k$ (0.3\\%) & $\\sim$100 & $\\sim$538 & 5.4$\\times$ \\\\\n309\tMax Jaccard ($J_{\\eapig{} \\times \\ep{}}$) & 0.307   & 0.042   & 7.3$\\times\\downarrow$ \\\\\n310\t\\css{}$= 1.0$ edges & 7 & 0 & --- \\\\\n311\t\\css{}$= 1.0$ CPR   & 0.109 & --- & --- \\\\\n312\t\\bottomrule\n313\t\\end{tabular}\n314\t\\caption{GPT-2 vs.\\ Qwen-2.5 edge space scaling. Jaccard drops superlinearly with edge space size, rendering consensus empty on the larger model.}\n315\t\\label{tab:scaling}\n316\t\\end{table}\n317\t\n318\t\\paragraph{IFR vs.\\ \\acdc{} as third method.}\n319\tWe tested both Iterative Feature Removal (IFR) and \\acdc{} top-$k$ as the third method (Table~\\ref{tab:qwen_third}).\n320\tBoth fail identically: IFR yields $J = 0.000$ with both gradient methods; \\acdc{} yields $J \\le 0.008$.\n321\tThe failure is not method-specific but structural.\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{table}[t]\n324\t\\centering\n325\t\\small\n326\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n327\t\\toprule\n328\t\\textbf{Third method} & $\\boldsymbol{J(\\cdot, \\text{\\eapig{}})}$ & $\\boldsymbol{J(\\cdot, \\text{\\ep{}})}$ & \\textbf{\\css{}$= 1.0$} \\\\\n329\t\\midrule\n330\tIFR   & 0.000 & 0.000 & 0 edges \\\\\n331\t\\acdc{} & 0.006 & 0.008 & 0 edges \\\\\n332\t\\bottomrule\n333\t\\end{tabular}\n334\t\\caption{Qwen-2.5 IOI: both candidate third methods produce near-zero overlap with gradient methods. The failure is independent of method choice.}\n335\t\\label{tab:qwen_third}\n336\t\\end{table}\n337\t\n338\t\\subsection{Qwen-2.5 0.5B: Arithmetic}\n339\t\n340\tThe pattern replicates on Arithmetic: $J_{\\max} = 0.038$, \\css{}$= 1.0 = 0$ edges, \\css{}$\\ge 2/3 = \\sim 55$ edges with CPR $= 0.025$.\n341\tTable~\\ref{tab:qwen_tasks} confirms that all Qwen tasks exhibit the same degenerate pattern.\n342\t\n343\t\\begin{table}[t]\n344\t\\centering\n345\t\\small\n346\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n347\t\\toprule\n348\t\\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{\\css{}$= 1.0$ edges} & $\\boldsymbol{J_{\\max}}$ & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n349\t\\midrule\n350\tIOI (IFR)             & 0 & 0.000 & FAIL \\\\\n351\tIOI (\\acdc{})         & 0 & 0.042 & FAIL \\\\\n352\tArithmetic (\\acdc{})  & 0 & $\\sim$0.038 & FAIL \\\\\n353\t\\bottomrule\n354\t\\end{tabular}\n355\t\\caption{All Qwen-2.5 0.5B configurations fail with the same pattern: near-zero Jaccard and empty consensus core.}\n356\t\\label{tab:qwen_tasks}\n357\t\\end{table}\n358\t\n359\t\\subsection{Combinatorial Dilution}\n360\t\n361\tThe mechanism is \\emph{combinatorial dilution}: when the edge space grows by $5.5\\times$ but circuit structure does not scale linearly, a fixed top-$k$ percentage selects non-overlapping subsets.\n362\tAt GPT-2 scale, top-0.3\\% yields $\\sim$100 edges from a 32k pool; at Qwen scale, $\\sim$538 edges from 179k.\n363\tThe IOI circuit distributes across more layers and heads in the larger model, and each method traces a different subset of the expanded computation paths.\n364\t\n365\tExtrapolating the Jaccard decay ($0.307 \\to 0.042$ over $5.5\\times$ edge space growth), models at Gemma-2 2B scale ($\\sim$500k edges) would yield $J_{\\max} \\approx 0.01$, and LLaMA-3.1 8B ($\\sim$2M edges) would approach random intersection baselines ($J \\approx 0.003$).\n366\tWe did not run these experiments, as the scaling trend is already clear.\n367\t\n368\t% ============================================================\n369\t\\section{Comparison with \\circus{}}\n370\t\\label{sec:circus}\n371\t\n372\t\\circus{} \\citep{parekh2026circus} constructs consensus \\emph{within} a single method by varying the pruning threshold.\n373\tWe compare both consensus approaches on GPT-2 tasks (Table~\\ref{tab:circus}).\n374\t\n375\t\\begin{table}[t]\n376\t\\centering\n377\t\\small\n378\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n379\t\\toprule\n380\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{IOI CPR} & \\textbf{SVA CPR} & \\textbf{Arith CPR} & \\textbf{Tasks $+$} \\\\\n381\t\\midrule\n382\t\\circus{}      & $-0.108$ & $-0.150$ & $-0.120$ & 0/3 \\\\\n383\t\\css{}$= 1.0$  & $+0.109$ & $-0.820$ & $-0.184$ & 1/3 \\\\\n384\tBest single    & $+0.029$ & $-0.348$ & $-0.131$ & 0/3 \\\\\n385\t\\bottomrule\n386\t\\end{tabular}\n387\t\\caption{Comparison with \\circus{} (zero ablation). Both consensus methods are severely limited. \\circus{} fails on all tasks; \\css{} succeeds only on IOI.}\n388\t\\label{tab:circus}\n389\t\\end{table}\n390\t\n391\t\\circus{} produces negative CPR on all three tasks, performing worse than random ablation.\n392\tIts within-method threshold sweep constructs nested sets ($k{=}50 \\subset k{=}100 \\subset k{=}150$) whose intersection equals the most conservative cut---it cannot leverage cross-method information.\n393\t\\css{} outperforms \\circus{} on IOI where methods genuinely disagree, but neither method provides reliable consensus on the majority of tasks.\n394\t\n395\t% ============================================================\n396\t\\section{Jaccard as a Diagnostic}\n397\t\\label{sec:jaccard}\n398\t\n399\tGiven that \\css{} fails when methods are too similar (high Jaccard) or too different (low Jaccard at scale), we examine whether pairwise Jaccard can serve as a \\emph{diagnostic} for when consensus is viable.\n400\t\n401\t\\begin{table}[t]\n402\t\\centering\n403\t\\small\n404\t\\begin{tabular}{lccll}\n405\t\\toprule\n406\t\\textbf{Task$\\times$Model} & $\\boldsymbol{J_{\\max}}$ & \\textbf{\\css{} $\\Delta$} & \\textbf{Quality} & \\textbf{Confounds} \\\\\n407\t\\midrule\n408\tIOI$\\times$GPT-2     & 0.307 & $+0.080$ & confounded & isinf + zero \\\\\n409\tSVA$\\times$GPT-2     & 0.653 & $-0.002$ & neutral    & $\\Delta \\approx$ noise \\\\\n410\tArith$\\times$GPT-2   & 0.695 & $-0.066$ & negative   & --- \\\\\n411\tIOI$\\times$Qwen      & 0.042 & undef.  & degenerate & \\css{}$= 1.0$ empty \\\\\n412\tArith$\\times$Qwen    & 0.038 & undef.  & degenerate & \\css{}$= 1.0$ empty \\\\\n413\t\\bottomrule\n414\t\\end{tabular}\n415\t\\caption{Jaccard--utility relationship across all task$\\times$model pairs. Only $n = 3$ valid points exist; $n = 2$ are degenerate.}\n416\t\\label{tab:jaccard_utility}\n417\t\\end{table}\n418\t\n419\tTable~\\ref{tab:jaccard_utility} reveals that with only 3 valid data points, no statistically significant correlation exists (Spearman $\\rho = -0.50$, $p = 0.67$).\n420\tThe sole positive data point (IOI$\\times$GPT-2) is confounded and serves as a Cook's $D > 5.0$ influential point that drives the entire negative trend.\n421\tThe Qwen data points are degenerate---\\css{} advantage is undefined when the consensus set is empty.\n422\t\n423\tRather than ``Jaccard predicts consensus utility,'' the data suggest a \\textbf{Goldilocks zone} hypothesis: consensus requires Jaccard high enough for non-empty intersection, yet low enough that the intersection carries information beyond any single method.\n424\tOur experiments show that the upper bound (too-high Jaccard) manifests on GPT-2 SVA/Arithmetic, while the lower bound (too-low Jaccard) manifests on Qwen.\n425\tThe viable zone---if it exists---is narrow and task-specific.\n426\t\n427\t\\begin{table}[t]\n428\t\\centering\n429\t\\small\n430\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n431\t\\toprule\n432\t\\textbf{Analysis} & $\\boldsymbol{\\rho}$ & $\\boldsymbol{p}$ & $\\boldsymbol{n}$ & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n433\t\\midrule\n434\tAll valid points            & $-0.50$ & 0.67 & 3 & not significant \\\\\n435\tRemove IOI (confounded)     & undef.  & ---  & 2 & cannot compute \\\\\n436\tInclude Qwen ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.05$ proxy) & $-0.30$ & 0.62 & 5 & not significant \\\\\n437\t\\bottomrule\n438\t\\end{tabular}\n439\t\\caption{Jaccard--utility correlation is not statistically significant under any analysis.}\n440\t\\label{tab:correlation}\n441\t\\end{table}\n442\t\n443\t% ============================================================\n444\t\\section{Discussion}\n445\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n446\t\n447\t\\paragraph{Why does IOI succeed?}\n448\tIOI on GPT-2 is a well-studied task with a known ground-truth circuit \\citep{wang2023interpretability}.\n449\tThe three methods discover genuinely different subsets of this circuit: \\eapig{} and \\ep{} favor different attention heads, and \\acdc{}'s greedy search identifies a distinct sparse backbone.\n450\tThe $J_{\\max} = 0.307$ reflects real algorithmic diversity, and the 7-edge consensus core captures high-confidence edges.\n451\tHowever, even this success is fragile: under mean ablation, the advantage drops to $+0.007$.\n452\t\n453\t\\paragraph{When methods are not independent.}\n454\t\\eapig{} and \\ep{} are both gradient-based and share the same underlying signal (gradient of the patching effect).\n455\tOn SVA, where number-tracking heads in layers 7--10 dominate, both methods converge on the same edges ($J = 0.653$).\n456\tTrue $K$-method consensus requires methods from genuinely different algorithmic families---a condition that is harder to satisfy than it appears.\n457\t\n458\t\\paragraph{The scaling wall.}\n459\tThe combinatorial dilution effect is perhaps the most significant finding.\n460\tEdge-level consensus fundamentally assumes that different methods can ``see'' the same edges.\n461\tIn larger models, the same behavioral circuit may be implemented through different edge-level paths, and each method discovers a different valid decomposition.\n462\tThis is not a failure of any individual method but a structural property of edge-level analysis in deep networks.\n463\t\n464\t\\paragraph{Implications for mechanistic interpretability.}\n465\tOur results suggest that edge-level binary consensus is not a viable path to more reliable circuit identification, at least not without addressing the independence and scaling challenges.\n466\tPossible directions include: (1) consensus at the \\emph{node} (attention head) rather than edge level, where combinatorial dilution is less severe; (2) soft consensus using calibrated scores rather than binary membership; and (3) consensus conditional on method family, explicitly accounting for inter-method correlation.\n467\t\n468\t% ============================================================\n469\t\\section{Related Work}\n470\t\\label{sec:related}\n471\t\n472\t\\paragraph{Circuit discovery.}\n473\tActivation patching \\citep{vig2020investigating,geiger2021causal} and its automated variants \\citep{conmy2023automated,syed2023attribution,hanna2024does} identify circuits by measuring the causal effect of interventions.\n474\t\\citet{wang2023interpretability} manually identified the IOI circuit in GPT-2.\n475\t\n476\t\\paragraph{Cross-method comparison.}\n477\t\\citet{mondorf2025beyond} compared gradient-based circuit discovery methods and proposed score averaging.\n478\tTheir work focuses on methods within the gradient family; we extend to cross-family consensus.\n479\t\\citet{parekh2026circus} proposed \\circus{}, a within-method consensus approach using threshold diversity.\n480\t\n481\t\\paragraph{Circuit faithfulness evaluation.}\n482\t\\citet{hanna2024does} introduced systematic faithfulness metrics.\n483\tOur ablation sensitivity analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:ablation_confound}) highlights that faithfulness measurements are sensitive to the choice of ablation method---a confound that affects all circuit evaluation, not just consensus approaches.\n484\t\n485\t% ============================================================\n486\t\\section{Limitations}\n487\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n488\t\n489\t\\paragraph{Limited task and model coverage.}\n490\tWe evaluate only 3 completed tasks on GPT-2 Small and 2 on Qwen-2.5 0.5B.\n491\tThe factual recall experiment was abandoned due to task--model mismatch (GPT-2 Small baseline accuracy $= 52\\%$), and larger models (Gemma-2 2B, LLaMA-3.1 8B) were not attempted due to the established scaling trend and hardware constraints.\n492\t\n493\t\\paragraph{Method family diversity.}\n494\tTwo of our three methods (\\eapig{}, \\ep{}) are gradient-based.\n495\tThis limits the effective diversity to $K \\approx 2$ in some settings.\n496\tA truly diverse ensemble would require methods from more distinct algorithmic families.\n497\t\n498\t\\paragraph{Sample size for correlation analysis.}\n499\tWith $n = 3$ valid data points, we cannot establish statistical relationships between Jaccard and consensus utility.\n500\tThe Goldilocks zone hypothesis remains speculative.\n501\t\n502\t% ============================================================\n503\t\\section{Conclusion}\n504\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n505\t\n506\tWe present a systematic empirical analysis of cross-method circuit consensus, evaluating \\css{} across multiple tasks and model scales.\n507\tOur central finding is negative: binary edge-level consensus fails in 4 out of 5 task--model configurations, and the sole positive case (IOI on GPT-2) is confounded by binarization artifacts and ablation method bias.\n508\t\n509\tThree structural factors explain the failure: (1) insufficient method independence, particularly between gradient-based approaches; (2) binarization pathologies that create misleading diversity signals; and (3) combinatorial dilution that renders edge-level consensus impossible at scale.\n510\t\n511\tThese findings carry a constructive message: before investing in consensus-based circuit refinement, practitioners should verify that their methods produce genuinely independent evidence---measurable via pairwise Jaccard---and that the edge space is small enough for meaningful overlap.\n512\tThe Goldilocks zone for viable consensus appears narrow, task-specific, and difficult to guarantee a priori.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n515\t\\bibliography{references}\n516\t\n517\t\\end{document}\n518\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013XjSyj7kThM1sVp2qFHJq9","session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"2afe9673-ff39-47c9-be01-185f886a950f","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:06:50.761Z"}
