{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013XjSyj7kThM1sVp2qFHJq9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nKey findings from the review:\n\n**What works:** The paper's honest negative-result reporting is commendable, the ACDC isinf binarization analysis is a genuine methodological contribution useful to the community, and the combinatorial dilution observation is conceptually important for anyone doing edge-level cross-method comparisons at scale.\n\n**What doesn't:** Seven weaknesses were identified, with the most critical being:\n\n1. **W1 -- Straw man problem:** Binary majority voting is too naive a consensus mechanism; its failure is predictable from first principles (expected intersection scales as J^(K-1)). The paper does not engage with why this should have been expected to work.\n\n2. **W2 -- Narrow scope:** Effectively 3 completed conditions on a single 117M model. Qwen experiments are degenerate (empty consensus sets). Factual recall abandoned, Gemma-2 and Llama-3.1 not attempted.\n\n3. **W3 -- Thin related work:** Only 9 references. Missing Marks et al. (Sparse Feature Circuits), Lieberum et al. (scaling of circuit analysis), Kramar et al. (AtP*), PIE, and others directly relevant to the paper's claims.\n\n4. **W4 -- Goldilocks zone is unfalsifiable:** n=3 valid points, Spearman rho=-0.50, p=0.67, yet it receives a full section and features in the abstract.\n\nThe paper would be better positioned as a shorter findings paper, or strengthened by testing more sophisticated consensus mechanisms (score fusion, node-level agreement) whose failure would be genuinely surprising."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ad024dd0de7e7b7a9 (use SendMessage with to: 'ad024dd0de7e7b7a9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 74388\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 165110</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"48c36c2a-8066-4b7e-9987-2edd998c2ff9","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:09:09.138Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty, Significance, Related Work, and Writing Quality**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Cross-Method Circuit Consensus Fails: An Empirical Analysis\" — an analysis paper that introduces Cross-method Structural consensus Scoring (CSS), a binary consensus framework for circuit discovery methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC). It evaluates CSS across 3 tasks on GPT-2 Small and 2 tasks on Qwen-2.5 0.5B, finding it fails in 4/5 configurations. The sole positive case (IOI on GPT-2) is confounded by ACDC isinf binarization and zero-ablation size bias. Three structural failure causes identified: (1) high inter-method correlation, (2) ACDC binarization pathologies, (3) combinatorial dilution at scale.\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages)\n1. **Introduction** — motivates circuit consensus, introduces CSS, previews predominantly negative findings and three contributions\n2. **Background and Method** (§2) — describes EAP-IG, EP, ACDC; defines CSS formula; evaluation metrics (CPR, CMD, Jaccard); binarization approaches\n3. **GPT-2 Small Experiments** (§3) — IOI (PASS), SVA (FAIL), Arithmetic (FAIL), Factual Recall (abandoned)\n4. **Binarization and Ablation Confounds** (§4) — ACDC isinf vs top-k analysis, top-k sweep, ablation method sensitivity (zero/mean/resample)\n5. **Cross-Model Scaling Failure** (§5) — Qwen-2.5 0.5B results, combinatorial dilution analysis\n6. **Comparison with CIRCUS** (§6) — both consensus approaches severely limited\n7. **Jaccard as a Diagnostic** (§7) — n=3 too small, Goldilocks zone hypothesis\n8. **Discussion** (§8) — why IOI succeeds, method independence, scaling wall, implications\n9. **Related Work** (§9) — circuit discovery, cross-method comparison, faithfulness evaluation\n10. **Limitations** (§10) — acknowledges limited coverage, method family diversity, sample size\n11. **Conclusion** (§11)\n\n## Related Work Cited\n- Olah et al. 2020 (Zoom In, circuits introduction)\n- Wang et al. 2023 (IOI circuit in GPT-2, ICLR)\n- Conmy et al. 2023 (ACDC, NeurIPS)\n- Syed et al. 2023 (EAP-IG / attribution patching, ATTRIB Workshop NeurIPS)\n- Hanna et al. 2024 (Edge Pruning / greater-than, NeurIPS)\n- Mondorf and Plank 2025 (cross-method comparison, arXiv)\n- Parekh et al. 2026 (CIRCUS, arXiv)\n- Vig et al. 2020 (causal mediation analysis)\n- Geiger et al. 2021 (causal abstractions)\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Binary edge-level consensus (CSS) fails in 4/5 task-model configurations\n2. The sole positive case (IOI/GPT-2) is confounded by two evaluation artifacts\n3. Three structural causes: method correlation, binarization pathologies, combinatorial dilution\n4. Goldilocks zone hypothesis: consensus needs Jaccard in a narrow range\n\n## Experiment Design Context\nThe original plan included experiments on Gemma-2 2B and Llama-3.1 8B (not attempted), Qwen Arithmetic (projected from IOI pattern), and a Jaccard-utility correlation analysis that yielded n=3 non-significant results. The narrative was pivoted from \"CSS as a method\" to \"analysis of why consensus fails.\"\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the research question interesting? Are the findings new? Does the analysis paper framing add value?\n2. **Significance**: Will the community care? Does this change how people do circuit discovery?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are key references missing? Is positioning accurate?\n4. **Writing quality**: Clarity, organization, flow. Is the negative-result framing effective?\n5. **Framing and narrative**: Is pivoting from \"method paper\" to \"analysis paper\" successful? Does the Goldilocks zone contribute or feel speculative?\n6. **Broader impact**: What does this mean for mechanistic interpretability?\n\nAlso consider:\n- Is the paper primarily a negative result about a relatively simple idea (binary voting)? Is this surprising enough?\n- The related work section is quite thin (only 9 references). What's missing?\n- The paper tests only one model family scale-up (GPT-2 → Qwen). Is this sufficient?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2: Novelty, Significance, and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered Q1, Q2, ...)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"ad024dd0de7e7b7a9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nKey findings from the review:\n\n**What works:** The paper's honest negative-result reporting is commendable, the ACDC isinf binarization analysis is a genuine methodological contribution useful to the community, and the combinatorial dilution observation is conceptually important for anyone doing edge-level cross-method comparisons at scale.\n\n**What doesn't:** Seven weaknesses were identified, with the most critical being:\n\n1. **W1 -- Straw man problem:** Binary majority voting is too naive a consensus mechanism; its failure is predictable from first principles (expected intersection scales as J^(K-1)). The paper does not engage with why this should have been expected to work.\n\n2. **W2 -- Narrow scope:** Effectively 3 completed conditions on a single 117M model. Qwen experiments are degenerate (empty consensus sets). Factual recall abandoned, Gemma-2 and Llama-3.1 not attempted.\n\n3. **W3 -- Thin related work:** Only 9 references. Missing Marks et al. (Sparse Feature Circuits), Lieberum et al. (scaling of circuit analysis), Kramar et al. (AtP*), PIE, and others directly relevant to the paper's claims.\n\n4. **W4 -- Goldilocks zone is unfalsifiable:** n=3 valid points, Spearman rho=-0.50, p=0.67, yet it receives a full section and features in the abstract.\n\nThe paper would be better positioned as a shorter findings paper, or strengthened by testing more sophisticated consensus mechanisms (score fusion, node-level agreement) whose failure would be genuinely surprising."}],"totalDurationMs":165110,"totalTokens":74388,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5548,"cache_read_input_tokens":68428,"output_tokens":411,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5548},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":411,"cache_read_input_tokens":68428,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5548,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5548,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
