{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NXNbcWBwjegw8TJrJmFWaK","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n**Reactions to R2's Review:**\n\n**1. Straw man critique (W1):** Strongly agree. R2 sharpens something I felt but didn't articulate cleanly. Binary majority voting's failure is mathematically predictable — intersection size decays exponentially with the number of methods. The paper needed to justify why this naive baseline was worth extensive testing, or jump directly to weighted/soft consensus. This reinforces my concern that the experimental design lacks rigor in baseline selection. It does change my assessment: the negative results are less informative than I initially credited, because the failure mode was foreseeable.\n\n**2. Thin related work (W3):** Yes, this matters for experimental positioning. Missing Sparse Feature Circuits (Marks et al.) and AtP* means the paper doesn't situate itself against the most relevant recent circuit-finding methods. My concern about unfair CPR size comparisons becomes worse when you realize the paper hasn't even acknowledged methods that might provide better-calibrated baselines. The 9-reference count is genuinely low for a paper claiming to survey consensus across circuit-finding approaches.\n\n**3. Score revision:** I revise down to **2/6 (Reject)**. R2's straw man point deflates the value of the negative results — the paper's main claimed contribution. Combined with my existing concerns (n=3 Jaccard correlation, no statistical testing, IOI dominance), the paper doesn't clear the bar. Better suited as a workshop paper with a more sophisticated consensus mechanism tested.\n\n**Final score: 2/6 (Reject)**"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a03b89dc1c067e337 (use SendMessage with to: 'a03b89dc1c067e337' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16247\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 16747</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"3d733385-714f-4835-a121-acdbe54596cb","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:09:44.721Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments focus) in a roundtable review. You initially scored the paper 3/6 (Weak Reject).\n\nYour initial key findings were:\n- Clean method formulation, honest negative results\n- No statistical testing (bootstrap, permutation) on primary CSS > best single claim\n- Unfair CPR comparison between circuits of vastly different sizes (7 edges vs 50 edges) without size-controlled baselines\n- IOI dominates positive evidence\n- Jaccard-utility correlation rests on only 3 valid data points (n=3, not 6)\n- Ablation method comparison is useful\n- CIRCUS comparison well-designed\n\nNow you are shown R2's review (Novelty + Related Work). R2 also scored 3/6 (Weak Reject). R2's key points:\n\n**R2 Strengths:** Honest negative-result reporting is commendable. ACDC isinf binarization analysis is a genuine contribution. Combinatorial dilution observation is conceptually important.\n\n**R2 Weaknesses:**\n- W1 (Straw man): Binary majority voting is too naive; its failure is predictable from first principles (expected intersection scales as J^(K-1)). Paper doesn't engage with why this should have worked.\n- W2 (Narrow scope): Effectively 3 completed conditions on a single 117M model. Qwen experiments degenerate. Multiple experiments not attempted.\n- W3 (Thin related work): Only 9 references. Missing Marks et al. (Sparse Feature Circuits), Lieberum et al., Kramar et al. (AtP*), PIE, etc.\n- W4 (Goldilocks zone unfalsifiable): n=3 points, Spearman rho=-0.50 p=0.67, yet gets a full section + abstract mention.\n- Better as shorter findings paper or needs more sophisticated consensus mechanisms tested.\n\n**Your task:** React to R2's review in 200 words max.\n1. Do you agree with R2's \"straw man\" critique? Does it change your assessment?\n2. Does R2's point about thin related work affect your evaluation of experimental positioning?\n3. Would you revise your score? If so, why?\n\nFormat: Brief reactions, then final score.\n","agentId":"a03b89dc1c067e337","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n**Reactions to R2's Review:**\n\n**1. Straw man critique (W1):** Strongly agree. R2 sharpens something I felt but didn't articulate cleanly. Binary majority voting's failure is mathematically predictable — intersection size decays exponentially with the number of methods. The paper needed to justify why this naive baseline was worth extensive testing, or jump directly to weighted/soft consensus. This reinforces my concern that the experimental design lacks rigor in baseline selection. It does change my assessment: the negative results are less informative than I initially credited, because the failure mode was foreseeable.\n\n**2. Thin related work (W3):** Yes, this matters for experimental positioning. Missing Sparse Feature Circuits (Marks et al.) and AtP* means the paper doesn't situate itself against the most relevant recent circuit-finding methods. My concern about unfair CPR size comparisons becomes worse when you realize the paper hasn't even acknowledged methods that might provide better-calibrated baselines. The 9-reference count is genuinely low for a paper claiming to survey consensus across circuit-finding approaches.\n\n**3. Score revision:** I revise down to **2/6 (Reject)**. R2's straw man point deflates the value of the negative results — the paper's main claimed contribution. Combined with my existing concerns (n=3 Jaccard correlation, no statistical testing, IOI dominance), the paper doesn't clear the bar. Better suited as a workshop paper with a more sophisticated consensus mechanism tested.\n\n**Final score: 2/6 (Reject)**"}],"totalDurationMs":16747,"totalTokens":16247,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1286,"cache_read_input_tokens":14606,"output_tokens":352,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1286},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":352,"cache_read_input_tokens":14606,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1286,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1286,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1}}}
