{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015fBPPW7ehqvzan3ieCF8TP","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Reaction to R1's Review\n\n**On size-controlled baselines:** R1's observation that CSS=1.0 achieves high CPR with only 7 edges versus 50-100 for single methods is devastating and directly reinforces my straw-man critique. The \"fusion beats individuals\" claim may simply be an artifact of intersection-driven sparsification, not genuine complementary discovery. If a size-controlled baseline (e.g., top-k edges from a single method matched to CSS edge count) achieves comparable CPR, the entire premise collapses. This moves the problem from \"straw man\" to potentially \"methodological confound.\"\n\n**On statistical testing:** The absence of bootstrap/permutation tests on the primary claim is worse than I initially weighted. Combined with my concern about n=3 Goldilocks points, the paper's central narrative rests on bare point estimates from a tiny experimental grid. No confidence intervals means we cannot distinguish signal from noise. This weakens my novelty assessment further — the ACDC isinf finding and combinatorial dilution concept remain genuine contributions, but they cannot carry a full paper when the headline claim is statistically unsupported.\n\n**On IOI dominance:** R1's point that 4/6 positive evidence points come from IOI aligns with my narrow-scope concern. One circuit is not a finding; it is a case study.\n\n**Score revision:** I maintain **3/6 (Weak Reject)**. R1's size-controlled baseline critique strengthens my concerns rather than alleviating them. The core contributions I acknowledged remain, but the paper's claims outrun its evidence."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7b8c4ea3693f6b65 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7b8c4ea3693f6b65' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16155\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 18295</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d450bbd-3b5c-4231-8573-8b79cc8ef481","uuid":"db73254e-0459-420f-8b14-deed774f4e7e","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:09:52.324Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 (Novelty + Related Work focus) in a roundtable review. You initially scored the paper 3/6 (Weak Reject).\n\nYour initial key findings were:\n- Straw man problem: binary majority voting failure is predictable\n- Narrow scope: effectively 3 conditions on one model\n- Thin related work (9 references, missing key papers)\n- Goldilocks zone hypothesis unfalsifiable with n=3\n- ACDC isinf analysis is a genuine contribution\n- Combinatorial dilution conceptually important\n- Better as shorter findings paper\n\nNow you are shown R1's review (Methods + Experiments). R1 also scored 3/6 (Weak Reject). R1's key points:\n\n**R1 Strengths:** Clean method formulation, honest negative results (especially Arithmetic), useful ablation method comparison, valuable IFR failure analysis, well-designed CIRCUS comparison.\n\n**R1 Weaknesses:**\n- No statistical testing on primary claim (CSS > best single method) — bare point estimates without bootstrap/permutation tests\n- Unfair CPR comparison between circuits of vastly different sizes (CSS=1.0 has 7 edges vs single methods with 50-100 edges) without size-controlled baselines\n- IOI dominates positive evidence (4/6 positive evidence points from IOI alone)\n- Jaccard-utility correlation on n=3 valid data points is statistically meaningless\n- Cross-model results lack error bars or multi-seed analysis\n\n**Your task:** React to R1's review in 200 words max.\n1. Does R1's size-controlled baseline point strengthen or change your straw-man critique?\n2. Does the lack of statistical testing on the primary claim change your novelty assessment?\n3. Would you revise your score? If so, why?\n\nFormat: Brief reactions, then final score.\n","agentId":"a7b8c4ea3693f6b65","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Reaction to R1's Review\n\n**On size-controlled baselines:** R1's observation that CSS=1.0 achieves high CPR with only 7 edges versus 50-100 for single methods is devastating and directly reinforces my straw-man critique. The \"fusion beats individuals\" claim may simply be an artifact of intersection-driven sparsification, not genuine complementary discovery. If a size-controlled baseline (e.g., top-k edges from a single method matched to CSS edge count) achieves comparable CPR, the entire premise collapses. This moves the problem from \"straw man\" to potentially \"methodological confound.\"\n\n**On statistical testing:** The absence of bootstrap/permutation tests on the primary claim is worse than I initially weighted. Combined with my concern about n=3 Goldilocks points, the paper's central narrative rests on bare point estimates from a tiny experimental grid. No confidence intervals means we cannot distinguish signal from noise. This weakens my novelty assessment further — the ACDC isinf finding and combinatorial dilution concept remain genuine contributions, but they cannot carry a full paper when the headline claim is statistically unsupported.\n\n**On IOI dominance:** R1's point that 4/6 positive evidence points come from IOI aligns with my narrow-scope concern. One circuit is not a finding; it is a case study.\n\n**Score revision:** I maintain **3/6 (Weak Reject)**. R1's size-controlled baseline critique strengthens my concerns rather than alleviating them. The core contributions I acknowledged remain, but the paper's claims outrun its evidence."}],"totalDurationMs":18295,"totalTokens":16155,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1286,"cache_read_input_tokens":14501,"output_tokens":365,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1286},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":365,"cache_read_input_tokens":